1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,080
When a light moves in a game, why can brightness remain on a wall
while a shadow follows the change?

2
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:11,220
We will connect DOOM’s sector brightness to Quake’s lightmaps,

3
00:00:11,220 --> 00:00:15,280
real-time shadows, and the ordering of forward and deferred
shading.

4
00:00:15,567 --> 00:00:22,820
You will be able to distinguish stored data from per-frame work and
identify where an image’s limitations come from.

5
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:25,720
Let us begin with a single surface.

6
00:00:26,567 --> 00:00:32,360
Making a pixel starts with identifying the visible surface.

7
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:37,340
The next task is estimating how much light that surface sends
toward the camera.

8
00:00:37,833 --> 00:00:41,353
Let us inspect that first stage in a Robo Recall diagnostic view.

9
00:00:41,713 --> 00:00:45,853
Diagnostic colors replace the usual material appearance to reveal
overlapping drawing.

10
00:00:46,193 --> 00:00:49,993
As the camera moves, the visible overlap of walls and railings
changes.

11
00:00:50,173 --> 00:00:53,093
These colors do not represent light intensity.

12
00:00:53,353 --> 00:00:57,973
We are separating visibility from lighting the visible surface.

13
00:01:04,567 --> 00:01:09,287
Rasterization projects triangles onto the screen and finds the
covered samples.

14
00:01:09,547 --> 00:01:13,467
A depth test selects the visible surface when triangles overlap.

15
00:01:13,817 --> 00:01:15,777
Now inspect an isolated wall.

16
00:01:16,197 --> 00:01:24,217
A broad face in the front view becomes a thin edge from the side
because the relation between camera and object has changed.

17
00:01:24,517 --> 00:01:28,577
Projecting triangles requires those positions and depths.

18
00:01:28,877 --> 00:01:33,117
Whether the wall is metallic and which light reaches it are separate
questions.

19
00:01:33,497 --> 00:01:39,577
This diagnostic view does not by itself reveal the GPU’s complete
execution order.

20
00:01:51,687 --> 00:01:54,027
Visibility alone does not calculate lighting.

21
00:01:54,227 --> 00:01:59,107
Position, surface normal, material, and light data are needed to
determine color.

22
00:01:59,447 --> 00:02:02,947
The normal describes a surface’s orientation.

23
00:02:03,267 --> 00:02:09,627
For a worked example, the dot product of an upward unit normal
and an upward light direction is one.

24
00:02:09,927 --> 00:02:13,047
At sixty degrees it is one half.

25
00:02:13,727 --> 00:02:18,927
In this simple diffuse model, with other factors unchanged, the
angular term is halved.

26
00:02:19,633 --> 00:02:23,467
Normalizing both vectors

27
00:02:24,107 --> 00:02:28,487
keeps their lengths from changing a comparison intended to
measure orientation.

28
00:02:29,087 --> 00:02:34,687
Visibility is a separate factor: even a perfectly aligned surface
receives no direct contribution from that light if the path is blocked.

29
00:02:35,287 --> 00:02:40,567
The rendering equation adds emitted light to reflected incoming
light.

30
00:02:41,367 --> 00:02:44,927
Material response and the cosine of the incident angle contribute to
that sum.

31
00:02:45,550 --> 00:02:51,027
Rather than deriving the entire integral, we will follow how games
divide this work.

32
00:02:51,527 --> 00:02:55,407
The directions they omit and the values they store affect the
resulting image.

33
00:03:01,550 --> 00:03:05,610
Rays can perform different tasks: finding a visible surface,

34
00:03:05,610 --> 00:03:11,490
testing visibility to a light, or finding a surface along a reflection
direction.

35
00:03:11,690 --> 00:03:15,250
A rasterized image can still use ray-based calculations.

36
00:03:15,590 --> 00:03:20,430
Rays may compute lighting before gameplay, while the running
game reads the stored result.

37
00:03:22,683 --> 00:03:28,839
Our first choice replaces complex light transport with a brightness
value assigned to each part of the world.

38
00:03:30,290 --> 00:03:35,430
DOOM’s basic 1993 lighting starts with a sector brightness value.

39
00:03:35,690 --> 00:03:41,950
The map assigns brightness to a sector, and the renderer uses it to
choose a color table.

40
00:03:44,687 --> 00:03:51,587
The source uses a bit shift of sector brightness to derive a light-table
index, with extra lighting added.

41
00:03:51,827 --> 00:03:55,007
It then applies wall-orientation adjustments and clamps the index.

42
00:03:55,387 --> 00:03:59,367
Horizontal and vertical walls receive different adjustments.

43
00:03:59,627 --> 00:04:05,327
This is an axis-based rule, rather than a continuous calculation of the
angle to a light.

44
00:04:05,533 --> 00:04:10,027
Two perpendicular walls can appear at different brightness in the
same sector.

45
00:04:10,187 --> 00:04:14,747
The sector has not changed; the wall orientation changes the
color-table selection.

46
00:04:15,067 --> 00:04:19,047
This is a simple orientation distinction rather than continuous
lighting over a curved surface.

47
00:04:19,269 --> 00:04:24,009
Distance-related behavior also enters through scale-dependent
color-table selection.

48
00:04:24,349 --> 00:04:28,309
Reducing the process to a single sector lookup would omit that
stage.

49
00:04:28,589 --> 00:04:32,209
This is not a per-light visibility test between an object and a lamp.

50
00:04:32,469 --> 00:04:37,989
The scheme alone does not automatically produce a local light
footprint or a precise object shadow.

51
00:04:38,289 --> 00:04:41,389
The footage is labeled as the 2024 rerelease.

52
00:04:41,389 --> 00:04:47,689
Later lighting added by a modern source port is not evidence of the
1993 implementation, even on the same DOOM map.

53
00:04:47,909 --> 00:04:53,289
We inspect the original sector-brightness and wall-orientation rules
separately in released source and our diagram.

54
00:04:54,267 --> 00:04:57,687
Quake stores more detailed lighting on surfaces.

55
00:04:57,947 --> 00:05:03,627
We distinguish the texture containing the wall’s appearance from
the lightmap containing lighting on that wall.

56
00:05:04,217 --> 00:05:07,617
As the viewpoint moves, the pattern stays attached to the surface.

57
00:05:07,897 --> 00:05:11,477
Keep its texture coordinates distinct from the coordinates used to
store lighting.

58
00:05:11,670 --> 00:05:16,210
Surface samples test visibility to lights.

59
00:05:16,470 --> 00:05:20,570
Orientation, distance, and light strength contribute to values
calculated before gameplay.

60
00:05:20,933 --> 00:05:25,050
Our diagram fills samples in sequence and transfers the results to a
texture.

61
00:05:25,370 --> 00:05:29,190
Samples behind the box are darker because the light is blocked.

62
00:05:29,583 --> 00:05:36,083
Carry the same question between rooms: which detail is the wall
pattern, and which is a broad lighting variation?

63
00:05:36,683 --> 00:05:41,063
If lighting was computed before play, the runtime needs an address
for finding the stored result.

64
00:05:41,303 --> 00:05:44,683
That is the role of the separate lightmap in our diagram.

65
00:05:50,450 --> 00:05:53,790
A wall texture may contain fine detail while its lightmap uses coarse
samples.

66
00:05:54,030 --> 00:05:57,810
Raising texture resolution alone does not refine the lighting
boundary.

67
00:05:58,010 --> 00:06:03,010
Raising lightmap resolution improves stored lighting detail without
inventing detail in the wall texture.

68
00:06:03,217 --> 00:06:05,970
A later engine demonstration makes the distinction observable.

69
00:06:05,970 --> 00:06:13,350
The 2017 Unreal comparison rooms are labeled with lightmap
resolutions of 128, 256, 512, and 1,024.

70
00:06:13,610 --> 00:06:15,790
Separate texture detail from lighting boundaries.

71
00:06:16,090 --> 00:06:21,630
The camera visits different authored rooms; this is not a
single-object on/off experiment.

72
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:25,440
The numbers in each room identify the displayed comparison
conditions.

73
00:06:25,740 --> 00:06:29,960
First inspect the wall pattern, then the boundary where a shadow
crosses it.

74
00:06:30,240 --> 00:06:34,940
The two kinds of detail need not be stored at the same density.

75
00:06:37,220 --> 00:06:40,080
At runtime, the renderer combines stored lighting with the surface
texture.

76
00:06:40,340 --> 00:06:46,440
Their coordinates and resolutions can differ; texture detail and
lightmap detail need not match.

77
00:06:46,850 --> 00:06:51,310
Inspect texture sharpness separately from lighting-edge sharpness.

78
00:06:52,220 --> 00:06:57,500
Quake II’s 1997 tools add reflected-light patch contributions to the
final lightmap samples,

79
00:06:57,680 --> 00:07:01,780
allowing baked indirect light to contribute beyond directly lit areas.

80
00:07:02,200 --> 00:07:04,720
Even a dark passage need not have a uniform color.

81
00:07:04,960 --> 00:07:09,540
Its appearance alone does not identify the algorithm used to
compute indirect lighting.

82
00:07:10,217 --> 00:07:14,070
This should not be labeled photon mapping.

83
00:07:14,350 --> 00:07:18,790
Algorithms can share the goal of storing indirect light while
calculating it through different processes.

84
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:23,460
A similar visual impression and an identical calculation method are
different claims.

85
00:07:25,217 --> 00:07:28,572
Thinking in separate layers explains update cost.

86
00:07:28,773 --> 00:07:32,633
Changing a wall texture and moving a light are different edits.

87
00:07:32,932 --> 00:07:35,192
The latter changes which samples receive illumination,

88
00:07:35,292 --> 00:07:39,052
so stored visibility and indirect lighting must be checked against the
new scene.

89
00:07:39,383 --> 00:07:43,523
Here the viewpoint travels along wall junctions.

90
00:07:43,983 --> 00:07:47,243
Compare the broad surface with the seam.

91
00:07:47,743 --> 00:07:53,843
A visible boundary can have several causes, including sample
density and surface partitioning.

92
00:07:54,163 --> 00:07:58,363
Identifying the cause requires the stored data and production
settings as well.

93
00:07:58,523 --> 00:08:01,843
Camera movement itself is not evidence of a new bake.

94
00:08:04,219 --> 00:08:06,179
Moving a light exposes the limitation:

95
00:08:06,259 --> 00:08:08,459
stored values describe the old configuration.

96
00:08:08,579 --> 00:08:13,179
New visibility and orientation require recalculation or an additional
dynamic-lighting method.

97
00:08:13,617 --> 00:08:19,677
This is object manipulation in the editor, not a completed light-off
and rebake comparison.

98
00:08:22,220 --> 00:08:25,300
A moving character is not attached to one wall location.

99
00:08:25,300 --> 00:08:30,120
Quake includes a downward query that samples surface lighting.

100
00:08:30,260 --> 00:08:33,720
Static surface storage and a moving object’s lighting query are
separate concerns.

101
00:08:34,600 --> 00:08:40,380
Moving the camera does not make stored lighting samples follow it.

102
00:08:40,700 --> 00:08:44,200
A moving object must retrieve values appropriate to its own
location.

103
00:08:44,400 --> 00:08:48,860
Shortly we will compare samples on a wall with samples distributed
through space.

104
00:08:55,467 --> 00:08:58,060
Texture lookup creates another problem.

105
00:08:58,260 --> 00:09:02,060
A distant screen pixel can cover many texels;

106
00:09:02,460 --> 00:09:06,420
sampling only one can make the pattern unstable as the camera
moves.

107
00:09:06,633 --> 00:09:11,793
Viewed obliquely, distant road details crowd into a narrow part of
the image.

108
00:09:12,053 --> 00:09:17,813
Selecting one texel as if it were a nearby surface cannot adequately
represent that larger region.

109
00:09:19,467 --> 00:09:22,827
Mipmaps store progressively smaller versions of a texture.

110
00:09:22,907 --> 00:09:28,867
Selecting an appropriate level avoids repeatedly resolving details
finer than the pixel’s footprint.

111
00:09:29,117 --> 00:09:32,097
Smaller mip levels combine fine details.

112
00:09:32,197 --> 00:09:37,737
The purpose is to choose values representing the sampled region,
not to select a blurry image arbitrarily.

113
00:09:38,270 --> 00:09:42,170
Bilinear filtering weights four neighboring texels within one level.

114
00:09:42,310 --> 00:09:47,383
Our diagram changes their weights as the sample moves between
them.

115
00:09:47,390 --> 00:09:51,010
Take a horizontal fraction of one quarter and a vertical fraction of
one half.

116
00:09:51,010 --> 00:09:56,890
The top-left and bottom-left weights are each three eighths; the
right-hand weights are each one eighth.

117
00:09:56,890 --> 00:10:01,250
They sum to one, and the nearer left values contribute more.

118
00:10:01,483 --> 00:10:04,663
A texture can hold data other than photographic color.

119
00:10:04,843 --> 00:10:08,263
This view separates masks packed into different channels.

120
00:10:08,503 --> 00:10:12,163
Red, green and blue do not automatically identify a normal map.

121
00:10:12,423 --> 00:10:16,683
Before interpreting filtering, identify what the blended values
actually mean.

122
00:10:20,552 --> 00:10:24,312
Trilinear filtering also blends between two adjacent mip levels.

123
00:10:24,473 --> 00:10:30,052
It smooths level transitions; it does not describe lighting along three
spatial axes.

124
00:10:30,152 --> 00:10:33,733
At an oblique viewing angle, a pixel’s footprint becomes elongated.

125
00:10:33,852 --> 00:10:39,632
Anisotropic filtering accounts for this directional shape instead of
using only a roughly square footprint.

126
00:10:39,867 --> 00:10:42,247
Follow the thin fire-escape edges.

127
00:10:42,487 --> 00:10:46,327
Their width in the image changes with the viewpoint.

128
00:10:46,587 --> 00:10:54,747
Stabilizing a distant thin structure resembles texture sampling, but
the problems are not identical.

129
00:10:55,047 --> 00:10:58,687
Texture filters handle the region read from stored detail.

130
00:10:58,867 --> 00:11:05,087
How much of a pixel an actual triangle edge covers is a question
about image samples.

131
00:11:05,087 --> 00:11:10,587
The later Forward example connects this to testing edges with
multiple samples.

132
00:11:25,733 --> 00:11:29,032
Filtering does not invent missing texture detail.

133
00:11:29,192 --> 00:11:35,312
It reads existing data more appropriately for screen samples, with
costs that depend on the chosen quality.

134
00:11:38,733 --> 00:11:43,787
Independent of the lighting model, processing order matters.

135
00:11:44,027 --> 00:11:49,467
Tile-based rendering divides the screen into smaller regions and
assigns triangles to them.

136
00:11:49,817 --> 00:11:53,837
Tilting the camera toward the building changes the geometry
covering the image.

137
00:11:54,097 --> 00:11:59,477
Classifying those triangles by the image region they affect is the
starting point for our tile explanation.

138
00:11:59,917 --> 00:12:04,277
This engine view is not an execution trace of historical PowerVR
hardware.

139
00:12:08,387 --> 00:12:12,527
The 1990s PowerVR lineage illustrates this hardware approach.

140
00:12:12,647 --> 00:12:17,887
We will not project the complete structure of a modern
programmable renderer onto those earlier devices.

141
00:12:18,250 --> 00:12:25,667
Keeping a tile’s working data close to the chip can reduce traffic to
larger external buffers.

142
00:12:25,967 --> 00:12:28,847
The tile is processed locally before its result is written out.

143
00:12:29,017 --> 00:12:34,789
In PowerVR’s TBDR terminology, deferred describes resolving
visibility before shading the required surfaces.

144
00:12:35,009 --> 00:12:40,369
This differs from the G-buffer-based deferred shading we will discuss
later.

145
00:12:40,617 --> 00:12:42,709
The list here contains primitives.

146
00:12:42,789 --> 00:12:47,609
Tiled lighting instead lists lights affecting a screen region.

147
00:12:47,669 --> 00:12:50,849
Similar-looking grids can contain different data for different
purposes.

148
00:12:50,889 --> 00:12:57,309
Skipping hidden opaque shading can help, but transparency,
discard operations, and intermediate geometry still have costs.

149
00:12:57,529 --> 00:13:02,029
Other GPUs also reduce work through early depth tests and caches.

150
00:13:02,787 --> 00:13:09,907
DOOM 3’s 2004 approach calculates direct-light and shadow
responses to moving lights and objects.

151
00:13:10,167 --> 00:13:13,607
This is a different update task from reading baked indirect lighting.

152
00:13:14,117 --> 00:13:17,037
Inspect the lit surfaces from the moving viewpoint.

153
00:13:18,787 --> 00:13:24,727
A simple diffuse model receives more direct light when its normal
faces the light.

154
00:13:25,027 --> 00:13:28,767
That example does not describe every material or effect in the game.

155
00:13:30,283 --> 00:13:33,063
A normal map stores small-scale surface orientation in a texture.

156
00:13:33,063 --> 00:13:40,143
It can produce rock-like shading without automatically changing a
flat surface’s outer silhouette.

157
00:13:40,150 --> 00:13:46,623
Official DOOM 3 game footage moves through dark industrial spaces
and shows illuminated characters and structures.

158
00:13:47,043 --> 00:13:53,343
We first observe that appearance, then examine shadow
construction through released source and our diagram.

159
00:13:53,343 --> 00:13:56,923
Game pixels alone do not reveal every stencil operation.

160
00:13:57,250 --> 00:14:01,310
While moving, distinguish surface brightness from the object’s
silhouette.

161
00:14:01,570 --> 00:14:04,350
The next diagram constructs the space behind that boundary.

162
00:14:05,157 --> 00:14:09,777
A shadow volume constructs the region behind an object from
boundaries seen by the light.

163
00:14:10,177 --> 00:14:15,197
The released source includes a step that connects original and
projected boundary points to form side faces.

164
00:14:15,537 --> 00:14:20,577
Stencil-buffer tests relate that volume to screen samples and
suppress the corresponding light contribution.

165
00:14:20,657 --> 00:14:25,877
The method tests a shadowed region rather than simply placing a
dark image on the floor.

166
00:14:26,017 --> 00:14:30,057
The stencil test is not simply painting the volume sides black.

167
00:14:30,317 --> 00:14:34,817
It counts how the test path crosses front and back faces

168
00:14:34,817 --> 00:14:39,817
to determine whether a sample is inside the shadowed region.

169
00:14:40,177 --> 00:14:44,797
Camera-inside cases also require careful counting direction and cap
handling.

170
00:14:44,917 --> 00:14:50,017
A point-light assumption produces sharp boundaries, and large
screen coverage can increase cost.

171
00:14:50,177 --> 00:14:55,097
Real-time direct lighting does not by itself solve indirect reflections
or color bleeding.

172
00:14:55,967 --> 00:14:59,247
Now inspect a robot surface in a more recent engine.

173
00:14:59,567 --> 00:15:03,367
Separate the outer silhouette from small variations inside it.

174
00:15:03,887 --> 00:15:08,887
Painted color, material reflection and surface orientation can all
affect the image.

175
00:15:09,287 --> 00:15:15,347
A bumpy appearance does not prove that the vertices have moved
into the same shape.

176
00:15:15,507 --> 00:15:19,587
This distinction leads into normals and stored lighting.

177
00:15:26,917 --> 00:15:32,490
Half-Life 2 illustrates another 2004 choice: richer use of baked
lighting.

178
00:15:32,850 --> 00:15:37,490
Responding to normal-map orientation requires more information
than one brightness value.

179
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:41,800
Notice the impression that the character and background occupy
one space.

180
00:15:42,040 --> 00:15:47,360
That does not mean static walls and moving characters must
retrieve lighting from the same storage.

181
00:15:47,720 --> 00:15:51,300
We will separate directional components from spatial samples.

182
00:15:53,920 --> 00:15:59,300
Valve’s contemporary presentation combines three basis-direction
lightmaps with a surface normal.

183
00:15:59,700 --> 00:16:05,000
Our three arrows represent stored directional components, rather
than three newly added lamps.

184
00:16:05,267 --> 00:16:12,440
The shown code weights each lightmap color by a saturated dot
product with its basis direction, then sums the results.

185
00:16:12,740 --> 00:16:17,100
We keep this version separate from variants that use squared
weights.

186
00:16:17,517 --> 00:16:21,097
The final image does not display directional lighting components
separately.

187
00:16:21,557 --> 00:16:26,937
The game shows the result; the diagram separates the intermediate
values producing it.

188
00:16:27,357 --> 00:16:30,737
Reading them together makes the storage scheme meaningful.

189
00:16:34,383 --> 00:16:36,920
Suppose the three stored directional colors differ.

190
00:16:37,200 --> 00:16:41,000
A normal facing the first basis increases that lightmap’s
contribution;

191
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:44,200
rotating the normal shifts the weights toward the other directions.

192
00:16:44,460 --> 00:16:49,680
Stored colors stay fixed while their contributions change, revealing
small surface detail.

193
00:16:50,367 --> 00:16:53,487
Inspect the changes in object position and orientation a little longer.

194
00:16:53,847 --> 00:16:59,187
First identify the face presented to the camera, then follow its
pattern and shading.

195
00:16:59,627 --> 00:17:03,107
Also distinguish object motion from camera motion.

196
00:17:03,467 --> 00:17:07,487
Our diagram changed only a normal direction to isolate one input.

197
00:17:07,907 --> 00:17:11,027
Several conditions may change together in a real editor.

198
00:17:11,387 --> 00:17:16,747
A brighter patch therefore does not reveal a numerical increase in a
particular lightmap component.

199
00:17:16,787 --> 00:17:23,147
Keep the three stages connected: surface orientation, stored data
and the retrieved result.

200
00:17:52,240 --> 00:17:55,020
Changing the normal changes the directional contributions.

201
00:17:55,220 --> 00:18:01,180
This adds small-scale orientation response beyond one flat
brightness value,

202
00:18:01,480 --> 00:18:05,480
without reconstructing geometry or every dynamic occlusion.

203
00:18:05,900 --> 00:18:10,580
In a game, camera and object motion can combine several changes.

204
00:18:10,880 --> 00:18:15,160
Isolating the effect of normals therefore requires a controlled
diagram.

205
00:18:15,500 --> 00:18:20,040
Compare the bright and dark detail within materials with their outer
silhouette.

206
00:18:20,360 --> 00:18:27,120
A richer way to read stored illumination does not automatically
create every moving object’s occlusion.

207
00:18:27,440 --> 00:18:33,800
Connect the finished scene to the calculation without guessing
invisible implementation details.

208
00:18:42,767 --> 00:18:48,160
Our spatial diagram separates a rock-like surface into actual
geometry and normal-map detail.

209
00:18:48,420 --> 00:18:53,760
Changing normal arrows affects internal shading while the outer
silhouette remains flat.

210
00:18:53,940 --> 00:18:57,480
Moving real vertices also changes silhouette and occlusion.

211
00:18:57,760 --> 00:19:00,520
The distinction is the information each representation creates.

212
00:19:01,300 --> 00:19:03,700
Here we inspect the robot’s arm and torso from different directions.

213
00:19:03,700 --> 00:19:12,240
The outline gives clues about geometry, while small variations inside
a face give clues about material and orientation.

214
00:19:12,760 --> 00:19:16,640
Treating them as the same thing overstates what normal maps can
do.

215
00:19:17,140 --> 00:19:20,740
Our earlier diagram changed directions without moving the plane’s
vertices.

216
00:19:21,400 --> 00:19:24,740
Apply the same habit of separating outline from interior detail here.

217
00:19:39,167 --> 00:19:41,980
Moving-object lighting stores and queries data differently.

218
00:19:42,200 --> 00:19:45,900
It needs spatial samples rather than a lightmap attached to a
surface.

219
00:19:46,240 --> 00:19:52,120
Half-Life 2’s ambient cube is not identical to every engine’s
spherical-harmonic probes.

220
00:19:52,500 --> 00:19:54,980
Look at the positions of the displayed points.

221
00:19:55,140 --> 00:19:59,820
They occupy the room rather than forming a wall-texture grid.

222
00:19:59,960 --> 00:20:05,460
Selecting the character lets us inspect the spatial relation between it
and the samples.

223
00:20:05,780 --> 00:20:10,760
A moving object needs lighting information for its new location.

224
00:20:10,920 --> 00:20:18,920
This follows our Half-Life2 discussion, but the Unreal demonstration
is a separate engine example, not the same storage format.

225
00:20:31,167 --> 00:20:38,040
A later Unreal lighting demonstration displays spatial samples and
moves a character between a dark room and a bright floor.

226
00:20:38,360 --> 00:20:42,860
A surface-texel grid and samples distributed through space store
information in different locations.

227
00:20:42,900 --> 00:20:49,980
This is a 2017 engine example of spatial lighting, not the identical
implementation of Half-Life 2 ambient cubes.

228
00:20:50,550 --> 00:20:54,210
First inspect the character together with the surrounding sample
visualization.

229
00:20:54,690 --> 00:21:01,010
Locate the prepared values, then follow the character as it changes
position.

230
00:21:01,410 --> 00:21:08,570
It cannot carry a single wall texel around the room; it needs lighting
information appropriate to its location.

231
00:21:08,910 --> 00:21:13,430
The room interior and the floor outside have different surroundings
and incoming illumination.

232
00:21:13,950 --> 00:21:19,390
As the character moves between them, inspect its body brightness
together with its position.

233
00:21:19,450 --> 00:21:26,610
The displayed samples are spatial clues for this lookup, not
necessarily actual light sources.

234
00:21:26,950 --> 00:21:30,390
Later, a changed viewpoint reveals the samples and object from
another direction.

235
00:21:30,650 --> 00:21:35,210
Two points close in the image may be separated in depth.

236
00:21:35,530 --> 00:21:39,770
Unlike a surface lightmap, this distribution must be read in height as
well.

237
00:21:40,090 --> 00:21:45,890
These operations do not measure exact interpolation weights or
per-frame cost.

238
00:21:46,150 --> 00:21:48,910
Internal values require separate engine evidence.

239
00:22:23,300 --> 00:22:25,800
Finally inspect the room from another viewpoint.

240
00:22:25,940 --> 00:22:30,660
Consider sample placement together with the places a character can
occupy.

241
00:22:30,920 --> 00:22:34,980
The existence of samples does not make lighting exact everywhere.

242
00:22:35,280 --> 00:22:39,960
Their density, placement and interpolation are further design
choices.

243
00:22:40,500 --> 00:22:45,120
The key here is that storage is in space rather than on the wall.

244
00:22:55,170 --> 00:22:58,670
Higher-quality baking consumes preparation time and storage.

245
00:22:59,090 --> 00:23:03,450
Lower runtime cost trades against production work and update cost.

246
00:23:03,850 --> 00:23:07,310
One map’s bake time cannot serve as a universal figure for games.

247
00:23:07,817 --> 00:23:14,437
For an editor, the question is not merely whether the image is bright
or dark, but what must be recomputed after a change.

248
00:23:14,737 --> 00:23:18,277
This room and wall inspection is part of scene authoring.

249
00:23:18,557 --> 00:23:23,077
It is not a baking benchmark, and its playback duration is not a
computation-time measurement.

250
00:23:32,173 --> 00:23:36,573
A remaining baked contribution after a light changes follows from
this assumption.

251
00:23:36,953 --> 00:23:41,213
Hybrid systems may update direct light dynamically while retaining
static indirect light,

252
00:23:41,593 --> 00:23:44,013
so the two update schedules must be examined separately.

253
00:23:44,873 --> 00:23:47,673
A shadow map stores depth from the light.

254
00:23:47,913 --> 00:23:55,073
It records the nearest visible surface, then transforms a
camera-visible point into the same light coordinates for comparison.

255
00:23:55,500 --> 00:24:02,713
With a depth convention where larger means farther, a tested point
behind the stored depth is considered blocked.

256
00:24:02,933 --> 00:24:06,473
Both values must use matching coordinates and conventions.

257
00:24:06,633 --> 00:24:13,573
For a numerical example, stored depth of 0.33 and receiver depth of
0.80

258
00:24:13,573 --> 00:24:16,253
place the receiver behind the first surface.

259
00:24:16,253 --> 00:24:23,593
If moving the blocker changes stored depth to 0.90, the receiver at
0.80 lies in front and is unoccluded by this test.

260
00:24:23,600 --> 00:24:26,873
Magnifying the light’s depth image reveals its texel grid.

261
00:24:26,873 --> 00:24:33,993
Covering a large region with few texels can create stair-stepped
shadow edges, even when the original shape is smooth.

262
00:24:34,273 --> 00:24:39,913
Small numerical differences between stored and reconstructed
depth can cause a surface to shadow itself.

263
00:24:40,073 --> 00:24:43,373
Bias adjusts the comparison to reduce this error.

264
00:24:43,800 --> 00:24:47,433
Excessive bias can detach the shadow from the object.

265
00:24:47,733 --> 00:24:52,173
A single chosen value is not guaranteed to solve every distance and
surface slope.

266
00:24:52,673 --> 00:24:58,013
PCF performs several neighboring depth comparisons and averages
their visibility results.

267
00:24:58,473 --> 00:25:01,933
Averaging depths first and comparing once is a different operation.

268
00:25:02,500 --> 00:25:07,996
If two of four neighboring tests report visibility, their PCF average is
one half.

269
00:25:07,996 --> 00:25:14,136
This produces an intermediate visibility value, but does not exactly
integrate every path from an area light.

270
00:25:14,336 --> 00:25:18,256
Wider filters require more samples and introduce further tradeoffs.

271
00:25:18,456 --> 00:25:23,256
PCSS searches for blockers and adjusts filter width using their
relation to the receiver.

272
00:25:23,256 --> 00:25:29,596
Variance Shadow Maps instead use depth moments and have their
own limitations, including light leakage.

273
00:25:29,600 --> 00:25:34,476
Variance Shadow Maps and Unreal’s Virtual Shadow Maps share an
abbreviation but are different methods.

274
00:25:34,656 --> 00:25:38,316
A later episode will distinguish virtual-page shadow management.

275
00:25:39,200 --> 00:25:43,453
One map covering a broad outdoor scene can make nearby shadows
coarse.

276
00:25:43,713 --> 00:25:49,193
Cascaded Shadow Maps split the camera’s view by distance and
assign shadow maps to those regions.

277
00:25:49,453 --> 00:25:51,493
This does not create more lights.

278
00:25:51,813 --> 00:25:57,633
It records shadows from one directional light at different spatial
densities near and far from the camera.

279
00:25:57,973 --> 00:26:03,333
For an illustrative example, 2,048 texels covering 20 meters gives
roughly one centimeter per texel.

280
00:26:03,553 --> 00:26:06,393
Covering 200 meters with the same count gives roughly ten
centimeters.

281
00:26:06,873 --> 00:26:09,693
Those numbers are not settings or performance measurements
from a game.

282
00:26:10,133 --> 00:26:14,133
They illustrate how coverage changes world-space texel size at the
same resolution.

283
00:26:14,600 --> 00:26:17,693
The point’s view depth selects a cascade.

284
00:26:18,193 --> 00:26:24,253
Blending near boundaries can soften transitions, while camera
movement may require stabilization relative to the texel grid.

285
00:26:24,453 --> 00:26:29,993
In the acquired official Unreal example, the character casts a visible
shadow while running on the floor, but not on the stairs.

286
00:26:30,333 --> 00:26:34,173
The documentation describes disabled CSM reception on the stairs.

287
00:26:34,400 --> 00:26:39,813
That clip demonstrates receiver configuration, rather than
visualizing cascade partitions.

288
00:26:40,173 --> 00:26:45,373
A separate spatial diagram explains the splits, while the observed
engine result illustrates the setting.

289
00:26:45,536 --> 00:26:49,556
A 2017 official Unreal demonstration shows the partitions more
directly.

290
00:26:49,756 --> 00:26:55,516
Enabling Show, Advanced, Shadow Frustums displays differently
colored projected boundaries over the scene.

291
00:26:55,796 --> 00:26:59,676
Observe how those boundaries change with the shadow distance
and distribution controls.

292
00:27:00,167 --> 00:27:04,707
The colored boundaries visualize shadow-recording regions, not
additional lights.

293
00:27:07,536 --> 00:27:13,896
The same demonstration changes the cascade count through four,
zero, one, and three while also adjusting shadow distance.

294
00:27:14,056 --> 00:27:17,896
Distinguish disabling cascades from assigning a broad region to one
map.

295
00:27:17,896 --> 00:27:23,516
Relate the one-versus-multiple edge comparison to our
equal-resolution coverage diagram.

296
00:27:23,656 --> 00:27:27,956
Several controls change, so we do not attribute every difference to
one parameter.

297
00:27:28,283 --> 00:27:30,883
Inspect near and far regions together.

298
00:27:31,163 --> 00:27:34,283
The total distance and distribution change as well as the number of
splits.

299
00:27:34,543 --> 00:27:42,103
Connect each visible setting change with the affected region instead
of drawing a conclusion from one still image.

300
00:27:44,539 --> 00:27:47,479
More cascades require additional maps and work.

301
00:27:47,699 --> 00:27:52,159
The technique allocates a limited budget while managing transitions
and instability.

302
00:27:52,917 --> 00:27:56,757
Even with shadow information, more lights increase work.

303
00:27:57,057 --> 00:28:03,097
Forward rendering evaluates material and relevant lighting as it
shades a surface.

304
00:28:03,483 --> 00:28:10,277
Deferred shading first stores visible-surface depth, normals, and
material data in a G-buffer.

305
00:28:10,557 --> 00:28:14,377
The lighting stage reads that data to accumulate light contributions.

306
00:28:14,967 --> 00:28:17,927
Here we inspect Robo Recall material data.

307
00:28:18,107 --> 00:28:21,387
Channel masks distinguish surface properties.

308
00:28:21,627 --> 00:28:26,527
Their colors do not automatically represent final display color or
normals.

309
00:28:26,747 --> 00:28:29,427
In Deferred shading, the important question is not just the number
of buffers,

310
00:28:29,427 --> 00:28:33,427
but what each value represents and what the next stage reads.

311
00:28:40,967 --> 00:28:45,367
Unwrapping the book places information from different faces onto
one plane,

312
00:28:45,627 --> 00:28:49,887
relating the original three-dimensional surfaces to texture
addresses.

313
00:28:50,287 --> 00:28:54,127
This does not mean storing only final lighting brightness.

314
00:28:54,447 --> 00:28:58,487
Separate data such as base color and roughness by their meaning.

315
00:28:58,927 --> 00:29:03,647
The export process then demonstrates handling those surface data
independently.

316
00:29:10,353 --> 00:29:15,253
An actual engine can inspect base color, normals, and roughness
separately.

317
00:29:15,253 --> 00:29:21,293
The 2017 Unreal material demonstration flattens a book model and
exports buffer data as separate images.

318
00:29:21,713 --> 00:29:25,433
This connects to distinct surface information rather than one final
shaded color.

319
00:29:25,833 --> 00:29:29,913
Baking materials or exporting buffers is different from baking
illumination.

320
00:29:30,273 --> 00:29:35,973
Suppose two lights contribute 0.25 and 0.35 to a shaded pixel.

321
00:29:36,173 --> 00:29:39,993
Forward shading adds them while processing the surface, producing
0.60.

322
00:29:40,253 --> 00:29:44,893
Deferred shading stores surface data first and accumulates the
same contributions later.

323
00:29:44,973 --> 00:29:49,153
The target result can match while intermediate data and ordering
differ.

324
00:29:49,333 --> 00:29:54,293
Four objects and three lights illustrate ordering, not a universal
workload.

325
00:29:54,633 --> 00:30:02,553
Culling can skip pixels outside a light’s influence, so not every
renderer evaluates every light at every pixel.

326
00:30:02,800 --> 00:30:07,613
S.T.A.L.K.E.R.’s development materials describe a deferred approach
for many lights.

327
00:30:07,933 --> 00:30:15,453
Its counts and performance figures belong to the documented scene
and hardware context.

328
00:30:15,833 --> 00:30:20,553
The benefit has costs: material data must be written and read,

329
00:30:20,553 --> 00:30:26,313
and one stored surface does not naturally represent several
translucent layers at a pixel.

330
00:30:26,500 --> 00:30:29,633
Place two glass layers in front of an opaque wall.

331
00:30:29,973 --> 00:30:36,673
A buffer storing only the nearest surface does not contain every
layer’s material.

332
00:30:36,673 --> 00:30:40,553
Compositing them needs additional information or passes.

333
00:30:40,553 --> 00:30:46,993
A real engine can therefore combine rendering paths within one
frame.

334
00:30:47,074 --> 00:30:51,173
Hybrid renderers may shade transparency in separate forward
passes.

335
00:30:51,534 --> 00:30:57,673
Light pre-pass is another compromise, computing lighting from
normals and depth before a later material pass.

336
00:30:58,017 --> 00:31:00,234
Forward rendering remains useful.

337
00:31:00,494 --> 00:31:07,614
Unreal’s Robo Recall example combines feature choices,
multisample anti-aliasing, and VR requirements.

338
00:31:07,954 --> 00:31:10,394
The relevant question is which work the implementation reduces.

339
00:31:11,283 --> 00:31:14,343
This is actual VR interaction with objects.

340
00:31:14,883 --> 00:31:17,183
Inspect both the response and the image edges.

341
00:31:19,450 --> 00:31:24,550
In the game running from the editor, hands, robots and fragments
move together.

342
00:31:24,770 --> 00:31:32,870
A clear image requires more than material brightness: thin
structures and moving outlines must also be sampled.

343
00:31:33,150 --> 00:31:39,430
This is an example of real renderer inputs, not evidence of a
particular frame rate.

344
00:31:51,450 --> 00:31:55,910
The comparison magnifies edges while handling multisample
settings.

345
00:31:56,350 --> 00:31:59,030
First locate the enlarged line in the original image.

346
00:31:59,410 --> 00:32:04,350
A single image sample may not adequately distinguish the area
covered by a thin triangle.

347
00:32:04,650 --> 00:32:08,430
Testing coverage at multiple positions can represent the boundary
differently.

348
00:32:08,750 --> 00:32:13,510
The magnified change alone does not establish a speedup.

349
00:32:13,750 --> 00:32:17,710
Sample count, resolution, materials and scene conditions all matter.

350
00:32:17,710 --> 00:32:22,470
Observe the console operations and edge changes without inferring
unreadable settings.

351
00:32:22,710 --> 00:32:27,250
This is a case of choosing a Forward path by considering quality,
required features and cost together.

352
00:32:53,320 --> 00:33:00,120
Missing screen-space features in a particular Unreal forward
implementation are implementation constraints,

353
00:33:00,520 --> 00:33:04,980
not proof that such effects are mathematically impossible in every
forward renderer.

354
00:33:05,500 --> 00:33:10,200
Changing diagnostic modes reveals information otherwise
combined in the final image.

355
00:33:10,460 --> 00:33:15,180
This reflection view is not automatically screen-space reflection.

356
00:33:15,440 --> 00:33:19,160
Even when effect names resemble one another, their inputs and
implementation must be identified.

357
00:33:25,317 --> 00:33:28,310
We also distinguish this from TBDR’s deferred terminology.

358
00:33:28,610 --> 00:33:34,990
That division concerns visibility before shading; this division
concerns material storage before lighting evaluation.

359
00:33:35,790 --> 00:33:39,910
Even with shadows, contact regions can appear flat.

360
00:33:40,250 --> 00:33:45,930
Screen-space ambient occlusion estimates nearby occlusion using
screen depth and neighboring samples.

361
00:33:46,283 --> 00:33:51,177
Nearby obstructions can reduce environmental illumination.

362
00:33:51,437 --> 00:33:57,937
SSAO approximates this with information already present on the
screen instead of tracing the complete scene in every direction.

363
00:33:58,057 --> 00:34:02,257
Our diagram places samples around a box corner and compares
depths.

364
00:34:02,497 --> 00:34:09,137
A small radius emphasizes contact; a larger one reaches farther but
can increase undersampling and incorrect associations.

365
00:34:09,337 --> 00:34:14,057
Filtering can reduce noise but may blur across boundaries.

366
00:34:14,117 --> 00:34:17,597
Sample count, filtering, and depth differences must be considered
together.

367
00:34:17,857 --> 00:34:21,257
The depth buffer primarily describes visible front surfaces.

368
00:34:21,297 --> 00:34:26,477
A thin rod in front of a distant wall does not reveal its thickness
through a single depth value.

369
00:34:26,477 --> 00:34:33,197
An excessive sample range can associate unrelated surfaces and
produce a dark halo around the object.

370
00:34:33,200 --> 00:34:39,137
CryEngine 2’s 2007 technical discussion and Unreal documentation
describe screen-based approximations and their limitations.

371
00:34:39,137 --> 00:34:42,337
Actual comparisons need identified versions and settings.

372
00:34:42,500 --> 00:34:49,057
A 2017 Unreal 4.16 demonstration approaches a cube model and
changes the AO Power and Bias controls.

373
00:34:49,157 --> 00:34:51,537
Watch the gaps between cubes and their floor contacts.

374
00:34:51,877 --> 00:34:56,697
Adjusting this contact treatment is different from adding a light
source.

375
00:34:57,000 --> 00:35:04,160
Several controls and the camera change in this sequence, so we will
not attribute every difference to one isolated number.

376
00:35:04,280 --> 00:35:10,720
First observe the actual manipulations and contacts; our separate
diagram isolates sample range and the limits of depth information.

377
00:35:13,000 --> 00:35:15,960
This is not a substitute for direct-light shadows.

378
00:35:15,980 --> 00:35:21,440
Darkening corners does not calculate occlusion for a particular lamp
or colored indirect light reflected from another wall.

379
00:35:21,467 --> 00:35:25,040
Information outside the screen and behind visible surfaces is still
missing.

380
00:35:25,200 --> 00:35:29,580
The same information boundary becomes important for
screen-space reflections in the next episode.

381
00:35:30,320 --> 00:35:37,000
This episode followed assigned brightness, stored surface lighting,
and updated visibility for moving lights.

382
00:35:37,220 --> 00:35:40,560
More lights also changed the ordering of material and lighting work.

383
00:35:40,717 --> 00:35:44,433
To distinguish methods, ask three questions: what is stored,

384
00:35:44,433 --> 00:35:49,793
which changes require recomputation, and which information is
missing from the input?

385
00:35:50,133 --> 00:35:54,413
The next episode extends the discussion to indirect light, reflections,
water, and fog,

386
00:35:54,533 --> 00:36:01,193
connecting photon mapping, SSR, Forward+, and clustered lighting
to the different problems they address.

387
00:36:01,500 --> 00:36:07,493
Reading techniques through their inputs and processing order
makes an engine frame easier to understand than names alone.

388
00:36:07,493 --> 00:36:12,293
The programming-coaching link in the description is available for
hands-on learning.
